Research Overview for "Small Littlewood-Richardson coefficients":

We develop structural insights into the Littlewood-Richardson graph, whose number of vertices equals the Littlewood-Richardson coefficient c({\lambda},{\mu},{
u}) for given partitions {\lambda}, {\mu}, and {
u}. This graph was first introduced by B"urgisser and Ikenmeyer in arXiv:1204.2484, where its connectedness was proved.   Our insights are useful for the design of algorithms for computing the Littlewood-Richardson coefficient: We design an algorithm for the exact computation of c({\lambda},{\mu},{
u}) with running time O(c({\lambda},{\mu},{
u})^2 poly(n)), where {\lambda}, {\mu}, and {
u} are partitions of length at most n. Moreover, we introduce an algorithm for deciding whether c({\lambda},{\mu},{
u}) >= t whose running time is O(t^2 poly(n)). Even the existence of a polynomial-time algorithm for deciding whether c({\lambda},{\mu},{
u}) >= 2 is a nontrivial new result on its own.   Our insights also lead to the proof of a conjecture by King, Tollu, and Toumazet posed in 2004, stating that c({\lambda},{\mu},{
u}) = 2 implies c(M{\lambda},M{\mu},M{
u}) = M + 1 for all M. Here, the stretching of partitions is defined componentwise.

